# HIS-9: the guard's --home exemption, both spellings.
#
# The generated bin/cli/skill-manager body, extracted from LauncherShims and
# driven directly with SKILL_MANAGER_HOME naming a DIFFERENT home.
#
#   case               exit   refused?
#   no flag            79     YES   <- the mismatch is refused
#   --home <x>          -     no    <- exempt
#   --home=<x>          -     no    <- exempt
#   --home <y>          -     no    <- exempt
#
# Both spellings are exempt, which is what HIS-12 (#161) needs: a remedy that
# carries --home is safe whatever SKILL_MANAGER_HOME the reader's shell exports.
#
# CAVEAT ON THIS PROBE, so nobody reads more into it than it shows: the
# extraction unescaped the backslash-backtick sequences that must stay LITERAL
# in the generated script, so the non-refused cases exit 1 on a spurious command
# substitution instead of reaching the pin. That is an artifact of this probe,
# not of the shim -- the real generated script keeps them escaped, and
# LauncherShimsTest drives the real one and asserts the pin runs. What this
# probe establishes is only the exit-79 column: refused without the flag,
# not refused with it, in either spelling.
